  "notes": [
    "the text was unwrapped from an RTF document, as a text editor saves a terminal program's capture: its groups, control words and escapes taken out, its paragraph and line breaks read as the download's lines; a line named is the document's",
    "`T` read in milliseconds, from the step: the median step between rows is 50, and the reader reads one above 5 as milliseconds (at the manuals' 1 to 33 samples a second, a step is 0.03 to 1 in seconds and 30 to 1,000 in milliseconds)",
    "`Alt` read in feet, from the manual: the Classic and TRS manuals (p37) state feet; the Quantum and Proton manuals give every height in feet and no Eggtimer manual offers meters",
    "`Veloc` read in feet per second, from the manual: the Classic and TRS manuals (p37) give the velocity in ft/sec, and the Proton's (p45) as the change in `Alt` over the step; it is the change in `Alt` over the step, within 1 % or 1 ft/s, in 13 of 13 rows after the first, where the reader asks 90 %",
    "`FAlt` read in feet, from the manual: it is the logger's filtered `Alt` (Quantum p38, Proton p45), in the same unit",
    "`FVeloc` read in feet per second, from the manual: the Proton's manual (p46) gives it as the change in the filtered altitude over the step, and the Excel guide (p7) graphs the velocity in ft/sec; it is the change in `FAlt` over the step, within 1 % or 1 ft/s, in 13 of 13 rows after the first, where the reader asks 90 %",
    "the marker columns, kept as written: `LDA`, `LowV`, `Apogee`, `N-O`, `Drogue` and `Main`; each is 0 but at the sample that marks its event, where the Quantum's and Proton's manuals (p38, p46) put the raw altitude, in feet",
    "`ASL Alt.` read in feet, from its unit mark: the flight summary writes 800 ft; the launch site's elevation above sea level, as the logger measured it (TRS manual p36)",
    "`Apogee` read in feet, from its unit mark: the flight summary writes 120 ft; the logger's apogee above its zero, kept beside the table's altitudes (TRS manual p36)",
    "`T @` and `Time @` read in seconds, from the manual: the TRS manual's download writes the flight summary's times in `secs.` (p36), and its LDA at 0.700 secs. as \"700ms into the flight\" (p37)",
    "the flight summary's times are its table markers' to the millisecond, one event each, the marker's: `Apogee` at `Apogee`, `LDA` at `LDA`, `N-O` at `N-O`",
    "the flight summary's times past the table's last, 0.65 s, kept as written and not read as events: no event of the flight comes after its last sample, so they aren't in seconds: `Unlock` 9500",
    "the flight settings, as the file writes them: `Drogue <1=Normal,2=Backup,0=OFF>` 1; `Main Altitude <50-2000 by 50, 0=OFF>` 300; `Bench mode {test}` 2",
    "1 line of the flight settings holds no setting: the name its owner gave the flight computer (TRS manual p24), left out of the record as free text that may name a person",
    "the flight summary's other lines, as the file writes them: `Base Temp: 61.0 °F`; `Last Flight Phase: 9` (landing detected, TRS manual p37); `Max Velocity: 440 ft/sec`",
    "1 lines before the title `Eggtimer … Flight Settings` weren't read",
    "the document holds 2 Eggtimer downloads one after another; the first was read, as a record holds one flight, and the titles of those that weren't are on lines 47"
